rds/event_name/545"} "Details"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The red-hot favorite in this event was " [:a {:href "/athletes/101557"} "An Hyeon-Su"] ". After his compatriot " [:a {:href "/athletes/100242"} "Kim Dong-Seong"] " had retired in 2002, An had become the dominant short track skater. He had won the overall title at the 2003, 2004 and 2005 World Championships, winning the 1,500 m on each occasion. With 2:10.639, the world record had also been in An’s hands since 2003."] "\n\n" [:p {} "One of An’s opponents, defending champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/101555"} "Apolo Anton Ohno"] ", was surprisingly eliminated in the semi-finals. The Koreans expressed regret over this, as they wanted to take revenge for Ohno’s win in the event back in 1,500, when Kim Dong-Seong had been controversially disqualified in favor of Ohno. But the Koreans did not care much, as they easily won the competition. An won the race, while his compatriot " [:a {:href "/athletes/109911"} "Lee Ho-Seok"] " finished second. Four-time Olympian " [:a {:href "/athletes/89605"} "Li Jiajun"] " grabbed his fifth Olympic medal, placing third. Later in the Games, An and Lee would repeat their 1-2 finish in the 1,000 m."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Helmet Nr"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Position Last Race"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Best Time"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "239"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/101557"} "Hyeon-Su An"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/KOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/KOR.png"}] "KOR"]] [:td {} "AF-1"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "240"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/109911"} "Lee Ho-Seok"]] [:td {} [:a {:hre